This repository is my capstone project for learning AI-native / agentic software engineering.

Learning roadmap:
docs/learning/master-learning-plan.md

Current learning/capstone state:
docs/learning/progress.md

Experiments:
docs/learning/experiments.md

Research:
docs/learning/deep-research-report.md

GitHub is the source of truth for current learning/capstone state.

When implementing learning tasks:

1. Read docs/learning/progress.md and relevant architecture first.
2. Read docs/learning/experiments.md when the task depends on prior experiment results.
3. Before substantial implementation, show a short proposed implementation plan.
4. Implement only the smallest real version needed to learn/test the current concept.
5. Do not implement future roadmap modules prematurely.
6. Prefer explicit, understandable architecture over unnecessary framework abstraction.
7. Respect the Topic Chat split between learning-critical code and delegatable boilerplate/plumbing.
8. Run required tests/checks.
9. Run the requested experiment when practical.
10. After implementation, give a short code tour:
    - 3–5 main files/functions the user should personally inspect;
    - the role of each;
    - the execution flow between them.
11. Update docs/learning/progress.md with:
    - module/task;
    - what was implemented;
    - important design decisions;
    - current result;
    - failures/open questions.
12. Update docs/learning/experiments.md when an experiment was run.
13. Do not mark a module complete merely because the code works.
14. Keep Git workflow simple until the Worktrees / Isolation module:
    module → implementation → tests/experiment → review/understanding → progress update → commit → push.
15. Do not introduce worktrees, complex branching, or parallel development before that module requires them.
16. After the user reviews the learning-critical code and the Topic Chat closes the module, commit and push the completed module state to GitHub.
